      "opening": "Read 2 Thessalonians 1:1–12 and mark grace, peace, thank, faith, growing, love, increasing, steadfastness, faith, persecutions, afflictions, evidence, righteous judgment, worthy, kingdom, suffering, repay, affliction, relief, revealed, angels, flaming fire, vengeance, punishment, eternal destruction, presence, glory, marvel, believed, worthy of calling, resolve for good, work of faith, power, name of Lord Jesus, glorified, grace. Ask what gives comfort to the afflicted and what responsibilities still belong to believers.",
      "context": "Paul, Silvanus, and Timothy begin by thanking God for faith that is growing and love that is increasing despite persecution. Their boasting in the church concerns steadfast faith, not the suffering itself. Verse 5’s language about being “considered worthy” of God’s kingdom should not be read as earning salvation by pain. The letter grounds Christian identity in grace and later prays that God’s power will fulfill good resolve and works of faith.\n\nVerses 6–10 use severe apocalyptic judgment imagery. The passage promises that those who afflict God’s people do not possess the final word, but interpreters differ over some details, including how to understand “eternal destruction” and the phrase related to the Lord’s presence. The text clearly places final justice with God at Christ’s revelation. It does not authorize revenge, cruelty toward opponents, or confident declarations about the eternal destiny of particular contemporary people.",
