      "context": "After describing deception, lawlessness, and judgment, the letter deliberately turns back to thanksgiving and reassurance. The Thessalonians are addressed as people “loved by the Lord.” Their stability begins not in their ability to decode prophecy but in God’s saving work through the Spirit, truth, and the gospel. The command to stand firm therefore grows from assurance rather than panic.\n\nVerse 13 contains a well-known textual variation reflected in translations: some manuscripts support language meaning “from the beginning,” while others support “as firstfruits.” The larger point remains that salvation is attributed to God’s gracious initiative and is experienced through sanctification by the Spirit and belief in the truth. Christians also differ over how divine election and human response relate. This brief paragraph should not be forced into one exhaustive philosophical system when its pastoral purpose is gratitude, assurance, and steadfastness.",
